 "drivingQuestion": "Is there another case where one speaks and brings an offering?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Blasphemer distinguished.",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "shevuot.22a.3",
   "daf": "22a",
   "sentenceId": 2,
   "he": "אָמַר לָהֶן: הֵיכָן מָצִינוּ בִּמְדַבֵּר וּמֵבִיא קׇרְבָּן, שֶׁזֶּה מְדַבֵּר וּמֵבִיא קׇרְבָּן. וְלָא?! וַהֲרֵי מְגַדֵּף! מְדַבֵּר וְאוֹסֵר קָאָמְרִינַן, וְהַאי מְדַבֵּר וְחוֹטֵא הוּא.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches that Rabbi Akiva «said to» the Rabbis: «Where do we find one who speaks and» is liable to «bring an offering» for it, «as this» oath taker merely «speaks,» i.e., takes an oath, «and brings an offering» for it? The Gemara asks: «And» do we «not? But isn’t a blasphemer» liable to bring an offering according to the opinion of Rabbi Akiva (see Karetot 7a)? The Gemara answers: «We are speaking of one who speaks and» in doing so «generates a prohibition. And this one,» the blasphemer, «is» merely one who «speaks and sins» but does not bring an offering."
  }
 ],
